[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a quick‑reference guide that shows **all the practical ways you can add a search / geocoder box to a Mapbox GL JS v2 map**, together with the trade‑offs you’ll face (license, cost, self‑hosting, UI framework support, etc.).  
Pick the option that best matches your budget, data‑source preferences, and how much you want to customise the UI.

---

## 1. The “official” Mapbox solution – **Mapbox GL Geocoder**

| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **Package** | `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` (npm) or a CDN script |
| **How it works** | Sends the query to the **Mapbox Geocoding API** (`https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/...`). Returns GeoJSON results that you can add to the map automatically. |
| **Token** | **Requires a Mapbox access token** (the same token you use for the map). |
| **Pricing** | Pay‑as‑you‑go on the Mapbox Geocoding plan (free tier: 100 k requests / month, then $0.75 per 1 k). |
| **License** | BSD‑3 (the plugin) + Mapbox proprietary API. |
| **UI** | Ready‑made dropdown, “search as you type”, optional “proximity”, “bbox”, “language”, “autocomplete”, “clear button”, etc. |
| **Installation** | ```bash\nnpm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder\n```\nor via CDN: `<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>` |
| **Basic usage** | ```js\nimport mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';\nimport MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';\nimport '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';\n\nmapboxgl.accessToken = '<YOUR_TOKEN>'; // <-- keep it!\nconst map = new mapboxgl.Map({container:'map',style:'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',center:[-122,37],zoom:12});\n\nconst geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken, mapboxgl: mapboxgl});\nmap.addControl(geocoder);\n``` |
| **Pros** | • Zero‑code UI – just drop the control.<br>• Full‑featured (autocomplete, fuzzy matching, language, proximity, bounding‑box, etc.).<br>• Works out‑of‑the‑box with Mapbox GL v2 (no extra polyfills). |
| **Cons** | • Ties you to Mapbox’s hosted service (proprietary, rate‑limited).<br>• You must keep a valid token (cost if you exceed free tier).<br>• Not usable if you later migrate to MapLibre without a Mapbox style. |

> **Bottom line:** If you’re already paying for Mapbox and you’re happy with their geocoding data, this is the fastest way to get a search box.

---

## 2. Open‑source / self‑hosted alternatives (no Mapbox token required)

All of the options below give you a **geocoder UI** (or a UI‑agnostic endpoint) that you can pair with Mapbox GL v2. They differ in data source, licensing, and how much you have to host yourself.

| Option | Data source | Hosting model | Pricing / limits | UI library (optional) | Typical integration steps |
|--------|-------------|---------------|------------------|-----------------------|---------------------------|
| **MapLibre‑GL‑Geocoder** (fork of Mapbox‑GL‑Geocoder) | Any **GeoJSON/TileJSON** source you point it at (e.g., **Nominatim**, **Photon**, **Pelias**, **MapTiler Geocoding**, **OpenCage**, **Geocode.earth**) | npm package, works in the browser; you still need a **search endpoint** | Free if you use a public endpoint (subject to that service’s policy) or self‑hosted (cost = server). | Same UI as Mapbox‑GL‑Geocoder (CSS compatible). | 1️⃣ `npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`  <br>2️⃣ `import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';` <br>3️⃣ Pass `apiUrl`/`fetch` options that point to your chosen service. |
| **Algolia Places** (now deprecated, but still usable) | Algolia’s hosted POI index (derived from OpenStreetMap) | CDN script, no server required | Free tier (5 k req/day) – **service is in maintenance mode**; not recommended for new projects. | Built‑in UI (autocomplete). | `new places({appId:'',apiKey:'',container:document.querySelector('#search')});` |
| **OpenCage Geocoder** | Aggregates OSM, OpenStreetMap, OpenAddresses, etc. | Hosted SaaS (API key) | Free tier 2 500 req/day, then $50/1 M. | No UI – you build your own input + fetch. | `fetch('https://api.opencagedata.com/geocode/v1/json?q='+encodeURIComponent(q)+'&key=YOUR_KEY')` |
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | OSM + proprietary enhancements | Hosted SaaS (API key) – also offers **self‑hosted Docker** image. | Free tier 100 k req/mo, then $0.5/1 k. | UI: `@maptiler/geocoder` (React, Vue, vanilla). | `import {Geocoder} from '@maptiler/geocoder'; new Geocoder({apiKey:'YOUR_KEY'}).addTo(map);` |
| **Photon** (by Komoot) | OSM data, hosted at `https://photon.komoot.io` | Public endpoint (rate‑limited) or self‑hostable Docker image. | Free for low‑volume; self‑host = server cost. | No UI – you write a tiny dropdown or use a generic UI lib. | `fetch('https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q='+q)` |
| **Pelias** | OSM + OpenAddresses + OpenStreetMap + custom data | Self‑host (Docker, Kubernetes) or use a managed provider (e.g., Mapzen, Pelias‑as‑a‑Service). | Free if self‑hosted; managed services have tiered pricing. | No UI – you can pair with **MapLibre‑GL‑Geocoder** or any custom UI. | `fetch('https://my-pelias/search?text='+q)` |
| **Nominatim** (OSM) | Pure OSM data | Public endpoint (`https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org`) (strict usage policy) or self‑host. | Free for low‑volume; self‑host = server cost. | No UI – you can use **MapLibre‑GL‑Geocoder** with `apiUrl: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search'` and custom `params`. | `fetch('https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&q='+q)` |
| **Geocode.earth** | OSM + proprietary enhancements | Hosted SaaS (API key). | Free tier 2 500 req/mo, then $0.5/1 k. | No UI – you build your own. | `fetch('https://api.geocode.earth/v1/search?text='+q+'&api_key=YOUR_KEY')` |

### 2.1. Quick‑start example with **MapLibre‑GL‑Geocoder + Nominatim**

```html
<link href="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<script type="module">
  import mapboxgl from 'https://cdn.skypack.dev/mapbox-gl@2.15.0';   // still using Mapbox GL v2
  import MapLibreGeocoder from 'https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder@1.5.0/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.min.js';

  mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';   // you still need this for the basemap
  const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
    // Nominatim endpoint – note the `format=geojson` param is added automatically
    apiUrl: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search',
    // Nominatim requires a `User-Agent` header; the library adds it automatically in the browser.
    // If you self‑host, you can add any extra query params here:
    // params: {countrycodes: 'us', addressdetails: 1}
    maplibregl: mapboxgl,   // tell the lib which GL object to use
    placeholder: 'Search places…',
    // optional: limit results, bias by map view, etc.
    limit: 5,
    // Nominatim does not support `proximity`, but you can add `viewbox` manually:
    // getViewbox: () => {
    //   const b = map.getBounds();
    //   return `${b.getWest()},${b.getSouth()},${b.getEast()},${b.getNorth()}`;
    // }
  });

  map.addControl(geocoder);
</script>
<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>
```

*Why this works:* `MapLibreGeocoder` is a **drop‑in replacement** for `MapboxGeocoder`. It only cares about the HTTP endpoint you give it, so you can point it at any service that returns **GeoJSON FeatureCollection** (Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, etc.). The UI stays the same, and you still get the nice “fly‑to result” behaviour automatically.

---

## 3. UI‑only libraries (you provide the backend)

If you already have a geocoding endpoint (e.g., you run your own Nominatim or Pelias) and you just need a **nice autocomplete dropdown**, you can use a generic UI component and then feed the results to the map yourself.

| Library | Framework | How you use it |
|---------|-----------|----------------|
| **autocomplete.js** (vanilla) | Vanilla JS | Create an `<input>` and attach `autocomplete` with a custom `source` that calls your endpoint. |
| **Awesomplete** | Vanilla | Same idea – lightweight, no dependencies. |
| **React‑Autosuggest** / **Downshift** | React | Build a controlled component that fetches from your geocoder and returns a list of suggestions. |
| **Vue‑Autocomplete** | Vue | Same pattern. |
| **Algolia InstantSearch** (can be pointed at any Algolia index) | React/Vue/Angular | If you index OSM POIs yourself in Algolia, you get a powerful UI. |
| **Leaflet‑Control‑Geocoder** (UI only) | Works with any map library (including Mapbox GL) | You can embed the UI and then manually add the result to the Mapbox GL map. |

**Typical flow**

1. **User types → UI component fires a fetch** to your geocoder endpoint (Nominatim, Pelias, etc.).
2. **Endpoint returns GeoJSON** (or plain JSON you transform).
3. **On selection**, you call `map.flyTo({center: [lng, lat], zoom: 14});` and optionally add a `Marker` or `Popup`.

```js
// Example with vanilla autocomplete.js
import autocomplete from 'autocomplete.js';

autocomplete('#search', { hint: false }, [
  {
    source: async (query, callback) => {
      const resp = await fetch(`https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&limit=5`);
      const data = await resp.json();
      // Photon returns `features` array already in GeoJSON format
      callback(data.features);
    },
    displayKey: 'properties.name',
    templates: {
      suggestion: (suggestion) => `<div>${suggestion.properties.name || suggestion.properties.street}</div>`
    }
